      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-2,3),$ and passes through the point $(6,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,11),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2pt \\\\ 4 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t.\n$$",
